// Bipartite transportation network simplex with an artificial root.
//
// Nodes:
//   0 .. n-1     sources, supply a[i]
//   n .. 2n-1    sinks, supply -b[j]
//   2n           artificial root, supply 0
//
// Initial basis: root connected to every node. Flow on root->source i is a[i]
// (root sends a[i] to source). Flow on sink->root is b[j].
// Artificial edge cost = ART (large). Real edges cost = M[i,j].
//
// We use a rooted tree at the artificial node.
// flow_down[v] = net flow from parent[v] to v. 
//   For root->source: flow_down = a[i] > 0
//   For root->sink:   flow_down = -b[j] < 0  (flow actually goes sink -> root)
//
// Entering edge is always a real source->sink edge (we never reintroduce artificial
// once removed; artificial may remain if... they should all be driven out because
// ART is large, except the 2n edges from root can't all leave — a tree on 2n+1 nodes
// has 2n edges. Initially 2n artificial edges. Each pivot replaces one artificial
// with a real edge until 2n-1 real edges + 1 artificial remain. The last artificial
// edge carries 0 flow (total supply of real nodes is 0) and stays. That's correct:
// the remaining artificial edge has 0 flow and doesn't affect the plan.
