            // only if vmax is the max v — hidden cost >= screen, red >= screen - u - v[c]
            // >= screen - u - vmax. If that >= min candidate dist, a hidden col cannot
            // beat the best candidate. But other candidates' dist may be inf, and a hidden
            // col could be second-best which Dijkstra needs. So this only skips full init
            // if hidden lb >= ALL current finite? No: hidden could be better than a bad candidate
            // but we need it if it's better than some dist we might use. Exact: hidden can
            // improve column c if screen - u - v[c] < dist[c]. With dist inf for non-candidates,
            // EVERY non-candidate has dist inf, so hidden ALWAYS can improve them unless we
            // don't care about inf. We DO care — a non-candidate might be the shortest path.
            // So init must full-scan unless we set dist of non-candidates to the hidden lower bound
            // and refine lazily. 
            // LAZY approach: set dist[c] = row[c]-u-v for candidates, and for others
            // dist[c] = screen - u - v[c] (lower bound). Mark them "bound" not exact.
            // When a bound column is selected as cmin, compute its true dist; if worse, reinsert.
            // That's a classic search with bounds. Let's do that — no separate full scan.
